stem, whether the reviewer pressed approve or reject; approval does the same),\\n  not with the reviewer\'s `reject_reason`, and `decided_by` is null (a system decision,\\n  as in approval). This is not an error: the answer is 200 with the claim. Signal (no\\n  extra field): the reviewed child order comes back with `reject_reason=ORDER_INVALID`.\\n- Else the child order becomes `rejected` with that `reject_reason` and `decided_by` the\\n  reviewer.\\nIn both outcomes `decided_at` is set and `row_version` goes up by 1; the order itself is\\nnot written. `reason` is written to claims.reason (the latest note) and to the audit log\\nwith the operator, the reject reason the reviewer chose and the outcome (including\\nORDER_INVALID). When every child order is decided the claim\'s `status` is\\nsummarized as BR-ATTR-18 says (any approved → approved; else any rejected → rejected;\\nelse cancelled); while one is pending it stays as it is. The applicant is told the result\\nby in-app message and push (BR-ATTR-18). The response is the whole claim after the\\nreview, the same shape as GET /admin/v1/claims/{claim_id}.\\n",\n        "x-auth": "admin",\n        "x-implementation": "planned",\n        "x-error-codes": [\n          10001,\n          10403,\n          20001,\n          20902\n        ],\n        "security": [\n          {\n            "adminBearerAuth": []\n          }\n        ],\n        "parameters": [\n          {\n            "$ref": "#/components/parameters/TraceId"\n          },\n          {\n            "$ref": "#/components/parameters/AdminClaimIdPath"\n          },\n          {\n            "$ref": "#/components/parameters/AdminClaimItemIdPath"\n          }\n        ],\n        "requestBody": {\n          "required": true,\n          "content": {\n            "application/json": {\n              "schema": {\n                "$ref": "#/components/schemas/AdminRejectClaimItemRequest"\n              },\n              "example": {\n                "expected_row